it turns out and there's a now quite a  history of this relatively recently  people have found that there are a lot  of species who can do number tasks by  number to us I mean tasks which depend  upon understanding the number of objects  in the display or what we would call  numerosity so you have to be very  careful here because you can't talk to  other species and they can't talk to you  so you can't explain to them what  aspects of the stimulus you want them to  respond to but you but you have to  design the the stimuli rather carefully  so that the critical aspect of the  stimuli is the numerosity of objects  actually before the Second World War a  brilliant German ethologist called Otto  Kerner had shown that corvids crows  jackdaws and so on had quite a good  number sense that they could match to  sample numbers up to about seven so he  showed them a sample of black blobs and  then they had to select one of a two or  three objects which had the same number  of blobs the blobs would be arranged  differently if they got it right they  got rewarded if they got it wrong they  didn't and they were pretty good at this  these corvatz since then there was a  been since the 30s there's been kind of  a hiatus in research on on animal  numerical cognition but in since the 90s  it's really started to take off again  and we now know that the whole range of  different animals couldn't can do this  both in the wild and in laboratories  particularly in the borough trees we  were interested in the counting  abilities of fish now small fish often  swim in shoals and if they are in a  shoal  there's a smaller risk of predation than  if they're by themselves and if they're  in a bigger show they've got a smaller  risk of predation than if they're in a  smaller shoulder safety in numbers the  safety in numbers exactly so you can use  this natural behavior of of these small  fish  and test it in the laboratory so you can  put them in a transparent tank with a  few fish on this side and a few more  fish on the other side and you see which  side they they swim to it's been shown  not by me but by my collaborators that  they will swim to the shoal of the side  of the of the tank with more fish in it  what we were interested to do in in a  paper we published this year was to see  whether they had a difference between  small number perception and large number  perception so in humans what happens is  that if you have to compare four versus  three four versus two four versus while  three versus two this is all equally  easy it doesn't depend on the ratio of  one number to the other so four against  one is just as easy as four against  three however once you get larger  numbers let's say eight against seven or  eight against five then there's a ratio  effect that is the bigger the difference  between those two numbers the easier it  is the question is would this be true of  Guppies which are a small fish about  that big relatively small brain and the  answer was yes that these small fish  didn't show a ratio effect when we you  ask them to compare four versus one as  compared to four versus three but for  larger numbers they did show a ratio  effect which suggests that like us they  have these two systems small number  system and a larger number system and we  thought this was really quite an  intriguing result I can imagine someone  at home thinking well surely the fish  just looks at two different groups of  fish or shoals of fish and can just  measure which ones bigger which one  takes up more volume and swims towards  that and it hasn't used any number sense  there it's just used and understanding  of how big something is how was the  experiments that you've been involved  with getting around that well it's very  difficult to control because not only  are there more fish over here there of  course swimming around  and you can have fewer fish over there  they're swimming around so it's really  quite difficult for the the the subject  fish to be able to make an estimate but  even though they do it actually rather  well how do we know that their enumerate  in the fish this side versus that side  well there are ways in which you can  manipulate the amount of fishness on  this side and the amount of fishness on  that side for example by putting  slightly larger fish on this side to  balance the number of fish on that side  that creates some problems because of  fish psychology which I not an expert in  like some fish prefer to swim in shoals  with larger fish and so you know that  creates complications however my  collaborators have done a nice training  experiment in which the fish doesn't  have to choose between two shoals  it has to select a gate into another  tank to join its friends and above the  gate will be a number of blobs and the  fish has to choose the right number of  blobs it would have had a training  session in which a certain number of  blobs would be rewarded and a different  number of blocks would not let say 3 is  rewarded 2 is not rewarded in the test  there be different blobs but there'll be  3 nests and tunas so different  arrangements of treatments and tunas and  the fish can learn this actually can  learn it quite well so there's now quite  good evidence that the fish can respond  to to number but of course they might  also in in the wild be responding to  other cues like the amount of fishiness  in in the neighborhood and of course  these are typically correlated with the  number and so you you want to try and  tease them apart experimentally but in  in real life of course they they they  tend to go together  how does understanding the mathematical  cognition or the number cognition of  gabbi's help anything why why have you  got interest in this well we want to  know what kinds of capacities the the  human brain inherits
 

